 # boundary. Discovery and emission stay serial in the parent.
+#
+# The pool must never hang silently. multiprocessing.Pool loses the task of a
+# worker that dies (killed for memory or by an antivirus, a crash in the
+# initializer) and imap then waits for ever with no output -- a player's build
+# sat at "Translate code 68%", one chunk short. So each worker has its own pipe
+# and the parent knows which chunk every worker holds:
+#  - a worker that dies (pipe broken / process gone) or raises: one line, the
+#    pool takes no new chunks, and the chunks still missing are lifted in this
+#    process by the same _worker_lift (same input, same code: same output);
+#  - no chunk finished for a while: a heartbeat line about every 30 s, and after
+#    _stall_limit() the busy workers are stopped and the rest is lifted here;
+#  - shutdown never waits without a limit: None to every worker, join with a
+#    timeout, and only a worker still alive after that is killed, alone (all
+#    results are in by then). No Pool.terminate(): on Windows it could hang
+#    joining a worker after all results were drained (main blocked for minutes
+#    at 100%, one idle worker left alive).
+# The default worker count also fits the free memory (_auto_jobs).
+# Test hooks (environment): T5DR_LIFT_TEST_KILL / T5DR_LIFT_TEST_HANG = chunk
+# numbers at which a worker dies / stops working, T5DR_LIFT_TEST_FREE_MB = the
+# free memory the default worker count sees, T5DR_LIFT_STALL_SECONDS and
+# T5DR_LIFT_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS replace the two intervals.
